course: dictionary
lesson: 12910
title: Words for things getting bigger
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe a change that happens step by step. Escalation is one of them. It means something grows bigger, worse or more serious, one stage at a time. «The argument began with a few rude words, then shouting, then pushing.» Each step made the problem bigger, so the argument escalated.
explain: Look at the word parts for clues. Escalate is the verb: to climb up, like a ladder. An escalator is a moving staircase that carries you up. So escalation is the climbing up of a problem or an amount. «Prices rose again this month, another escalation in the cost of food.» Here the amount keeps going up.
explain: The opposite is de-escalation, which means calming things down before they get worse. «The teacher stepped in early, which helped the de-escalation of the playground fight.» When de- starts a word, it often means undoing or reversing something.
fact: Escalation means becoming bigger, worse or more serious, step by step.
fact: Escalation can also mean a rise in amounts or prices, such as «an escalation in the cost of food».
fact: «Escalation of violence» means violence that keeps getting worse, from shouting to pushing to fighting.
fact: «De-escalation» means calming things down so that a situation does not get worse.
fact: Escalate is the verb: a quarrel can escalate, and an escalator carries you up a staircase.
exercise: «Shouting turned to pushing, and then to a fight.» What is this an example of? | Apologising | A quiet agreement | *Escalation | De-escalation
exercise: «The teacher stepped in quickly, which helped the ___ of the argument.» Which word fits? | Celebration | *De-escalation | Escalation | Exaggeration
exercise: «Prices rose again this month, another escalation in the cost of food.» What is getting bigger here? | A new kind of food | *The cost of food | The shop closing early | The food being thrown away
test: «The two groups began by insulting each other, then threw things, and the escalation made it dangerous.» What does escalation mean here? | *Getting worse step by step | Staying exactly the same | Being lost in the rain | A sudden end to the argument
test: Which word means calming things down before they get worse? | Escalator | Escalation | Exclamation | *De-escalation
exam: Which sentence uses escalation correctly? | «The sleepy cat was an escalation of the quiet afternoon.» | *«The rise in phone prices was a steady escalation over the year.» | «The fire was put out, which was a great escalation.» | «She wrote a long letter about her favourite colour.»
